## Finding

PR #2164 (merged, merge commit e61c8e6891fb, fixes issue #1964) replaced every `secret-tool` child-process keyring read with the new in-process helper `lookupSecretService` (`packages/ai/src/providers/secret-service.ts`). In doing so it dropped a fallback all three replaced call sites carried: when `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS` was absent from the environment, the old code synthesized `unix:path=/run/user/<uid>/bus` and passed it to the `secret-tool` child process.

The new helper performs no such synthesis, and it swallows every failure to `undefined`:

```ts
export const lookupSecretService = async (service: string, username: string): Promise<string | undefined> => {
  if (process.platform !== "linux") return undefined
  try {
    const { AsyncEntry } = await import("@napi-rs/keyring")
    const entry = new AsyncEntry(service, username, { linux: { store: "secret-service" } })
    const value = await entry.getPassword(AbortSignal.timeout(15_000))
    return value || undefined
  } catch {
    return undefined
  }
}
```

## Evidence

- PR #2164 diff: the removed fallback appears in all three replaced readers — `packages/ai/src/providers/vibe-auth.ts` (defaultKeyringLookup), `packages/ai/src/providers/antigravity-auth.ts` (keyring read), and `packages/ai/src/quota/providers/antigravity.ts` (keyringAuth) — each built `env.DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS = unix:path=/run/user/${uid}/bus` when unset before invoking `secret-tool`.
- Current main 0f5561c21cb84465cefef3881fa1d6f8eaa43a4d (verified 2026-11-12 via contents API): `secret-service.ts` is byte-identical to the PR version above (no DBUS handling), and all three callers (`vibe-auth.ts:140-141`, `antigravity-auth.ts:95-96`, `quota/providers/antigravity.ts:91-92`) route through it; no `DBUS` reference remains in any of the three files.
- The only test added for the helper (`packages/ai/test/provider/secret-service.test.ts`) pins that an explicitly bogus bus address resolves `undefined`; no test covers the absent-env case the old fallback handled.

## Why it matters

The Secret Service backend of `@napi-rs/keyring` reaches the session bus over D-Bus. A process launched without a session-bus environment (cron, a non-user systemd unit, a detached/ssh-spawned server — contexts the fleet itself runs servers in) previously still read the keyring via the synthesized address; now the same launch silently resolves no credential: vibe-auth falls through to its "no credential" error and both antigravity paths report the credential absent. The failure is silent by construction (catch → undefined), so the regression would present as intermittent missing credentials depending on how the process was started, not as an error pointing at the bus.

Note: whether the native binding's own bus discovery (e.g. an XDG_RUNTIME_DIR fallback in its D-Bus stack) already covers some of these launches is part of what triage must establish — the old code did not rely on it, and it does not cover launches where XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is also unset (cron).

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Triage establishes, with a test or a documented probe, whether `lookupSecretService` finds a real Secret Service entry when `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS` is unset (both with and without `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` set).
- [ ] If any of those launches fail: restore the address synthesis inside `lookupSecretService` (set `process.env.DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS` to `unix:path=/run/user/<uid>/bus` when unset, before constructing the entry), so all three callers regain the pre-#2164 behavior in one place.
- [ ] A regression test covers the absent-env synthesis path (e.g. asserts the synthesized address is in effect for the lookup, or that a lookup against the user's real bus path succeeds where the bogus-address case returns undefined).
- [ ] Existing secret-service / vibe-auth / antigravity-auth tests keep passing; the unavailable-bus case still resolves `undefined` rather than throwing.
